  r"The bound \(P_e\le(M-1)\beta^d\) of Lecture 1 asks for a large minimum distance \(d\), and a structure that helps the decoder. A large \(d\) alone is easy, the repetition code has \(d = n\), but it costs rate: \(R = 1/n\).",
  r"A binary linear \((n,k)\) code is a subspace of dimension \(k\) of \(\mathbb{F}_2^n\): \(x^n = u^kG\) and \(x^nH^{\mathsf T} = 0\). Systematic form, after reordering the positions (an equivalent code): \(G = [I_k\ \ A]\), \(H = [A^{\mathsf T}\ \ I_{n-k}]\).",
  r"The minimum distance \(d\) is the smallest weight of a nonzero codeword, and the smallest number of columns of \(H\) that add up to zero; \(d\le n-k+1\) (Singleton bound). The Hamming code takes all the nonzero columns of \(m\) bits: \(n = 2^m-1\), \(k = n-m\), \(d = 3\).",
  r"BSC: the syndrome \(s^{n-k} = y^nH^{\mathsf T} = e^nH^{\mathsf T}\) depends on the errors only, and ML decoding flips a coset leader. BEC: ML decoding solves the linear system \(x_EH_E^{\mathsf T} = y_KH_K^{\mathsf T}\), which works if and only if the erased columns of \(H\) are linearly independent.",
  r"The Hamming codes are perfect: the balls of radius 1 around the codewords fill \(\mathbb{F}_2^n\), so no code of the same length with \(d\ge 3\) has more codewords (the Hamming bound, exercise 8). On a general channel, soft ML decoding computes \(2^k\) costs; a hard decoder keeps about half of the distance.",
  r"LDPC codes have a sparse \(H\) and are decoded one check at a time: peeling on the BEC, message passing on a general channel. The cost is linear in \(n\); the decoder is not ML, but with long codes it comes close to capacity.",
