  {"title": "The price of distance",
   "body": r"<p>In the Gaussian example of Lecture 1, a coded bit is sent as the level \(\pm\sqrt{E_s}\) in Gaussian noise of variance \(N_0/2\), and \(\beta=e^{-E_s/N_0}\). (a) A code of rate \(R=k/n\) spends the energy \(nE_s\) on \(k\) information bits. Give the energy per information bit \(E_b\) and show that \(\beta^d=e^{-R\,d\,E_b/N_0}\). What is the exponent without coding, with \(R=1\) and \(d=1\)? (b) Deduce that, at the same \(E_b\) and at high \(E_b/N_0\), the bound \(P_e\le(M-1)\beta^d\) is better than uncoded transmission when \(R\,d&gt;1\). The number \(10\log_{10}(R\,d)\) is called the <em>asymptotic coding gain</em>, in dB. Compute it for the \((7,4)\) Hamming code and the \((3,2)\) single-parity-check code. (c) For the \((n,1)\) repetition code, compute \(R\,d\). Show that the absence of gain is not a weakness of the bound: ML adds the \(n\) observations \(y_i=(-1)^x\sqrt{E_s}+z_i\), and \(\frac{1}{\sqrt n}\sum_{i=1}^n y_i\) is distributed as one uncoded observation with the energy \(nE_s=E_b\). (d) On a BSC\((\delta)\) that is given once and for all, coding does not change \(\delta\). What does a larger distance cost then? (e) At the same bit rate, how many more uses of the channel per second, that is, how much more bandwidth, does a code of rate \(R\) need? When is the comparison of (b) the right one, and when is it not?</p>"},
